  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Mobility of alkali ions in aqueous solution&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#160;size of hydrated ion&#160;</mtext></mfrac></math></p><p>The mobility of alkali ions is inversely proportional to the size of ion in hydrated state, because bigger ion can&#39;t move faster. Also, small ion get hydrolysed to large exent due to which its size get increased. In other words, smaller ion will form hydrated ion of larger size.</p><p>Hence, correct order of mobility is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Rb</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#62;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#62;</mo><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#62;</mo><msup><mi>Li</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math></p>",
